<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Kirghiz ASSR</h1><p class="st-meta">1924 CE – 1936 CE · capital Frunze</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The autonomous Soviet republic that gave the Kyrgyz their first modern territorial state, the forerunner of today's Kyrgyzstan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the national delimitation of 1924 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyz-people">Kyrgyz</a> lands of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turkestan-assr">Turkestan ASSR</a> were made the Kara-Kirghiz Autonomous Oblast within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian Soviet Federative Socialist Republic</a>. In 1925 it was renamed the Kirghiz Autonomous Oblast and in 1926 raised to an Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishkek">Pishpek</a>, renamed Frunze.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic introduced Soviet administration, schools and a written <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyz-people">Kyrgyz</a> language, first in Arabic and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> script. Collectivisation and settlement of nomadic herders from 1929 brought hardship and revolt, and many fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Leading Kyrgyz communists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusup-abdrakhmanov">Yusup Abdrakhmanov</a> were purged in the 1930s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the 1936 Soviet constitution the republic became a union republic, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kirghiz-ssr">Kirghiz SSR</a>. Its borders, drawn in 1924, left large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajik</a> communities inside the republic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyz-people">Kyrgyz</a> communities outside it.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kirghiz-assr.jpg" alt="Kirghiz ASSR" width="320" height="215"><figcaption>Image: K. Ramzin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_delimitation_in_Middle_Asia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1924 CE – 1936 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Frunze</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yusup Abdrakhmanov</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kyrgyz, Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kara-Kirghiz Autonomous Oblast, Kyrgyz ASSR</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1930&amp;focus=kirghiz-assr" aria-label="Kirghiz ASSR on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6482 655 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kirghiz ASSR lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6802 911L5820 533M6802 911L6803 911"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5820 533h0M6803 911h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6802 911h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6802 911h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1930&amp;focus=kirghiz-assr">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
